\section*{Limitations}
\begin{enumerate}[leftmargin=*,itemsep=2pt]
\item \textbf{Model coverage.} Primary experiments use Qwen3-32B; cross-model validation (LLaMA-8B, LLaMA-70B, Gemini Flash) covers Dimensions~1--2 (count curves, BCS-controlled extremes) but does not replicate secondary analyses (error taxonomy, prompt sensitivity, TOST equivalence). Specifically, TOST factorial interaction (\S\ref{sec:multi_channel}) and SDGD failure analysis (\S\ref{sec:sdgd}) are established on Qwen3-32B alone; error taxonomy replicates on two additional models (Table~\ref{tab:cross_model_error_taxonomy}), but TOST and SDGD cross-model replication remains the most pressing extension. Gemini provides only partial, leverage-sensitive corroboration (leave-one-out $r{:}\;{-}0.80 \to {-}0.42$; Appendix~\ref{sec:appendix_cross_model_gemini}); proprietary-model generalization remains open.

\item \textbf{Effect size and statistical design.} Pairwise SCS explains a small fraction of type confusion variance; entity frequency, prompt position, and mention ambiguity likely account for the remainder. The count $\times$ BCS interaction is bounded on FewRel but nonzero on DocRED, partially confounded by BCS range restriction at higher counts (Appendix~\ref{sec:appendix_factorial}); logit-scale evidence remains inconclusive. DocRED count curves use 5 batches per level due to higher per-batch evaluation cost. BCS-controlled experiments use a single seed with greedy decoding; residual variance is small (Appendix~\ref{app:variance-decomposition}) but precise magnitudes may vary. Greedy decoding (temperature${}\,=\,0$) yields residual SD${}\,=\,0.88$\,pp, approximately 18--44\% of the observed BCS effect size, suggesting effect detection is robust but point estimates carry meaningful uncertainty; the factorial interaction point estimate (0.47\,pp) is far inside the $\pm 3$\,pp equivalence margin.

\item \textbf{Task scope.} Format independence is confirmed via paired constrained/unconstrained comparison (Appendix~\ref{app:format-independence}). BCS batch-level prediction is task-dependent: significant in RE but not detected in NER at current power, where errors scatter across miss, boundary, and type modes rather than concentrating in type substitution. A post-hoc relaxed-matching evaluation confirms that relaxing the span criterion does not recover the BCS signal (Appendix~\ref{app:relaxed-matching}), ruling out evaluation stringency as the explanation. The NER batch-level BCS null is observed on three models (8B, 32B, 70B; Table~\ref{tab:cross_model_ner_null}) but not validated on Gemini; the null could in principle differ for proprietary models.

\item \textbf{Causal mechanism and data contamination.} The study establishes correlational evidence with controlled stratification; mechanistic analysis at the attention or logit level is needed to confirm the causal pathway. A post-hoc renaming experiment (Appendix~\ref{app:renaming}) suggests SCS may proxy deeper conceptual overlap rather than surface similarity. FewRel (2018) may appear in pretraining corpora, but train-origin relations show lower accuracy than val-origin, opposite to the contamination hypothesis; DocRED (2019) provides a lower-contamination-risk replication (Appendix~\ref{app:split-origin}).
\item \textbf{Domain and language scope.} All evaluations use English-language datasets derived from Wikipedia or knowledge bases (Wikidata, FrameNet). Whether confusability effects hold for domain-specific schemas (e.g., biomedical NER, legal RE) or languages with different naming conventions is untested. BCS-controlled batches inherit the domain distribution of the source schema; domain-specific type subsets may shift the confusability--accuracy relationship. Sample sizes (1{,}000 instances per dataset) are sufficient for our primary effects but the pairwise SCS correlation approaches the detection floor.
\end{enumerate}

\paragraph{Across-batch correlation.} Batches share prompt template and LLM, introducing potential across-batch correlation; however, type composition is independently sampled per batch, and bootstrap CIs are computed over this independent unit of variation.

\paragraph{Multiple comparisons.} Few-NERD, FewRel, and DocRED draw from overlapping knowledge bases (Wikipedia, Wikidata), limiting dataset independence; we treat cross-dataset consistency as converging evidence rather than independent statistical replication \citep{rothman-1990} (noting that within-study prompt comparisons involve correlated observations rather than independent tests). Within-dataset secondary analyses involve multiple looks; we report individual $p$-values and note that core claims rest on $p < 0.001$ \citep{dror-2018-significance,card-2020-power}. The core claims (count decline, BCS--accuracy correlation) are robust to Bonferroni correction (all $p < 0.001$); the logit-scale interaction ($p = 0.047$) is the only result near the threshold and is already characterized as marginal.

